      {
        "id": "u3",
        "kind": "CONCEPT",
        "label": "Origin of the term alkaline earth metals",
        "attributes": {
          "depth": "explanation"
        },
        "subtopic_ids": [
          "s2"
        ],
        "rationale": "Explains the etymology of the name based on the basic nature of their oxides and historical usage of the term 'earths'.",
        "review_flags": [],
        "contextualization": {
          "value": "everyday",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "passage_id": "p12",
              "quote": "like soap or antacids"
            }
          ]
        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p11",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### Why Are They Called “Alkaline Earth” Metals?",
            "start": 487,
            "end": 535
          },
          {
            "id": "p12",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "- Their **oxides** (compounds with oxygen) are **alkaline** — they make water basic (like soap or antacids).",
            "start": 536,
            "end": 644
          },
          {
            "id": "p13",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "- Long ago, scientists called these oxides “earths” because they didn’t dissolve easily in water and looked like soil or rock.",
            "start": 645,
            "end": 771
          },
          {
            "id": "p14",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "- So the name stuck: “alkaline earth metals.”",
            "start": 772,
            "end": 817
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "Accurately recounts the historical etymology: oxides of these metals form basic (alkaline) solutions and were historically referred to as 'earths' because of their rock-like, sparingly soluble properties.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "u4",
        "kind": "CONCEPT",
        "label": "Key properties and periodic trends of alkaline earth metals",
        "attributes": {
