        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p15",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### Key Properties (What They’re Like)",
            "start": 819,
            "end": 857
          },
          {
            "id": "p16",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Alkaline earth metals share these traits:",
            "start": 858,
            "end": 899
          },
          {
            "id": "p17",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "1. **Two valence electrons**  ",
            "start": 901,
            "end": 931
          },
          {
            "id": "p18",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "   Each atom has two electrons in its outer shell. They tend to lose both electrons to form +2 ions (for example, Mg²⁺ or Ca²⁺). This is why they’re less reactive than alkali metals, which only have to lose one electron.",
            "start": 932,
            "end": 1152
          },
          {
            "id": "p19",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "2. **Shiny, silvery-white metals**  ",
            "start": 1154,
            "end": 1190
          },
          {
            "id": "p20",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "   When freshly cut, they look bright and metallic. They conduct heat and electricity well.",
            "start": 1191,
            "end": 1282
          },
          {
            "id": "p21",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "3. **Reactivity with water and air**  ",
            "start": 1284,
            "end": 1322
          },
          {
            "id": "p22",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "   - They react with water to produce hydrogen gas and a metal hydroxide, but much more slowly than sodium or potassium.  ",
            "start": 1323,
            "end": 1445
          },
          {
            "id": "p23",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "   - Calcium reacts noticeably with water; magnesium needs hot water or steam; beryllium hardly reacts at all.",
            "start": 1446,
            "end": 1556
          },
          {
            "id": "p24",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "4. **They form basic oxides and hydroxides**  ",
            "start": 1558,
            "end": 1604
          },
          {
            "id": "p25",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "   Their oxides and hydroxides turn red litmus paper blue — a classic sign they are bases.",
            "start": 1605,
            "end": 1695
          },
          {
            "id": "p26",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "5. **Trends as you go down the group**  ",
            "start": 1697,
            "end": 1737
          },
          {
            "id": "p27",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "   - Atoms get bigger.  ",
            "start": 1738,
            "end": 1762
          },
          {
            "id": "p28",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "   - It becomes easier to lose the two outer electrons.  ",
            "start": 1763,
            "end": 1820
          },
          {
            "id": "p29",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "   - So reactivity **increases** from beryllium (least reactive) to radium (most reactive).",
            "start": 1821,
            "end": 1912
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "Accurately explains the valence electron configuration (two outer electrons), formation of +2 ions, metallic luster, conductivity, reaction with water producing metal hydroxide and hydrogen gas with increasing reactivity down the group, basic nature of oxides/hydroxides, and the atomic size/ionization trend.",
          "errors": []
        }
